Dataset Splitting
=================

Purpose
-------

Split drone and non-drone recordings into training, validation, and testing
datasets before window generation.

Splitting is performed at the recording level to prevent data leakage caused by
overlapping windows.

---

Implementation
--------------

The dataset is divided into three subsets:

- Training Set
- Validation Set
- Test Set

The default split ratios are:

```text
Train      : 80%
Validation : 10%
Test       : 10%
```

Drone recordings and non-drone recordings are shuffled independently and split
using the same ratios to maintain a balanced dataset.

A fixed random seed is used so that every execution produces the same dataset
split.

---

Why Split Before Window Generation?
-----------------------------------

Window generation creates overlapping samples from a recording.

If the recordings are first converted into windows and then randomly split,
neighbouring windows may appear in different datasets while sharing many of the
same IQ samples.

This causes data leakage because the model is evaluated on data that is highly
similar to the data it was trained on.

To avoid this problem, recordings are first assigned to either the training,
validation, or testing dataset. Window generation is then performed separately
for each dataset.

---

Why Shuffle the Recordings?
---------------------------

Recordings are shuffled before splitting to eliminate ordering bias.

Without shuffling, recordings stored in a particular order could result in an
unbalanced dataset.

Shuffling ensures that each dataset contains a representative mixture of
recordings.

A fixed random seed guarantees that the same recordings are assigned to the
same dataset across multiple executions, making experiments reproducible.

---

Why 80 / 10 / 10?
-----------------

The current dataset contains a limited number of recordings.

Using an 80 / 10 / 10 split maximizes the amount of training data while still
providing separate validation and testing datasets.

The split ratios can be adjusted as additional recordings become available.

---

Why Use ValueError Instead of assert?
-------------------------------------

Function arguments are validated using exceptions rather than assertions.

Assertions are intended for debugging and internal consistency checks and may
be disabled when Python is executed with optimization.

Raising a ValueError ensures invalid input is always detected regardless of the
execution environment.

---

Testing
-------

The dataset splitting implementation is verified using unit tests.

The tests ensure that:

- Every recording appears exactly once.
- No recording exists in multiple datasets.
- The total number of recordings is preserved.
- Drone and non-drone recordings are split independently.
- Invalid split ratios raise a ValueError.
